  ;; ---- re-frame.story — the CLJS ARM of a SPLIT-HOST FACADE (rf2-i6kh) ----
  ;;
  ;; The rf2-i6kh sweep classified the 116 JVM-visible `re-frame.story`
  ;; exports and left these FIVE unrowed, which its own post-merge audit
  ;; caught: `re-frame.story` is a split-host `.cljc` exactly like
  ;; `re-frame.hicasso` below, and its `#?(:cljs …)` arm publishes five more
  ;; facade fns the JVM `ns-publics` cannot see. Four of them
  ;; (`registered-substrates`, `mount-shell!`, `unmount-shell!`,
  ;; `active-shell`) were merely SILENCED on the
  ;; `:story-spec-known-unmanifested` allowlist above; `register-substrate!`
  ;; was not even that, because it is not a var-row of
  ;; tools/story/spec/API.md, so the projection check never asked about it.
  ;; The rows below end both, and this PR adds the missing var-row too.
  ;;
  ;; `:facade? true` is carried PER ROW, the same second route the Xray block
  ;; above uses and for a related but not identical reason: `gen/facade?`
  ;; DOES name `re-frame.story`, but it is consulted only on the
  ;; JVM-introspected path (`build-rows`), so a CLJS-arm row never reaches
  ;; it. Blank these five flags and the facade-audit invariants stop seeing
  ;; the five vars while `--check` stays green — which is precisely the gap
  ;; the audit found.
  ;;
  ;; `:tier :tooling` and `:owner "007"` throughout, matching the 116 rows
  ;; these five belong beside: Story is a tooling PRODUCT, so per
  ;; spec/Conventions.md §Story / Xray nuance tooling legitimately IS its
  ;; front porch and the discriminator is the user's workflow.
  ;;
  ;; ALL FIVE ARE `:action :keep`, and no move was available to rule.
  ;; The audit's own wording left the branch open ("add register-substrate!
  ;; to the Story API projection OR otherwise explicitly classify/move it"),
  ;; so it was tested rather than assumed: the move discriminator asks
  ;; whether an existing owning namespace is already a clearer door AND
  ;; ordinary users do not need the name. The doors exist
  ;; (`re-frame.story.ui.multi-substrate`, `re-frame.story.ui.shell`) but the
  ;; second half fails on every one of the five — each is called by ordinary
  ;; HOST BOOT code, through the facade, in this repo's own testbeds and
  ;; docs. Moving them would also split two pairs (register/query,
  ;; mount/unmount+read) across a door boundary, which is the incoherence
  ;; §Keep product facades coherent forbids.
  ;;
  ;; `:runtime-verified? true` on all five is honest as of this PR and not a
  ;; hand-set boolean: the CLJS enumeration probe now `:require`s
  ;; `re-frame.story` and holds it `:fully-rowed`, so a rename or removal of
  ;; any of the five turns `npm run test:cljs` RED, and
  ;; `runtime-verified-rows-are-actually-covered` fails if this flag is ever
  ;; true without that coverage.
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "register-substrate!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The host-boot verb of the multi-substrate feature: a host calls it once per substrate it wants Story to render against, which is ordinary consumer workflow rather than chrome-internal plumbing. tools/story/spec/002-Runtime.md §Substrate hooks and 005-SOTA-Features.md §Multi-substrate side-by-side rendering are its normative home; the hicasso_counter testbed calls it through the facade at boot; story_multi_substrate_cljs_test pins rf.story/register-substrate! as THE public form (the sub-namespace call is the internal one); and skills/re-frame2 names the same qualified spelling when explaining why story-mcp list-substrates is browser-only. It is also the WRITE half of the pair whose READ half (registered-substrates) already sat on the facade -- keeping the reader and moving the writer is the split §Keep product facades coherent forbids."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "registered-substrates" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The READ half of the substrate pair: the set of registered substrate ids, which is how a custom shell or a tooling caller enumerates what a variant's :substrates opt-in may legally name. Already a var-row of tools/story/spec/API.md §Registry queries and of docs/story/api/reference.md, so it was documented as a facade surface while carrying no manifest row at all -- the exact accretion the facade audit exists to stop. Kept with register-substrate! as one workflow: register at boot, enumerate later."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "mount-shell!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "Front porch for a tooling product: the one call that puts Story on screen, and for most hosts the second line of their Story entry namespace after configure!. tools/story/spec/003-Render-Shell.md §Shell lifecycle is its normative home; tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle and docs/story/api/reference.md row it; docs/story/index.md shows it in the host entry point and Tutorial-Playwright.md assumes it as the page-root mount. CLJS-only because mounting a Reagent tree at a DOM node has no JVM equivalent -- that reader conditional is why the JVM generator never saw it, and says nothing about its status."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "unmount-shell!" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The teardown half of the mount pair Conventions §Lifecycle-verb law names, and idempotent by contract: what a host calls when it leaves Story mode, and what a test fixture calls between shell mounts. Rowed in tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle. Splitting it from mount-shell! would split one two-call workflow across two requires."}
  {:namespace "re-frame.story" :var "active-shell" :kind :fn :facade? true :tier :tooling :owner "007" :status "post-v1 lib" :runtime-verified? true
   :action :keep
   :justification "The READ of the shell pair -- the live shell handle or nil. Load-bearing rather than cosmetic: 003-Render-Shell.md §Shell lifecycle makes v1 a singleton in which a second mount-shell! tears the first down, so a host or fixture that must not clobber an existing shell has to ask first, and this is the only way to ask. Rowed in tools/story/spec/API.md §Shell lifecycle and docs/story/api/reference.md."}

