 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; :jvm-only-classification — the MIRROR of :cljs-only above, and the
 ;; smaller of the two by design.
 ;;
 ;; :cljs-only carries rows the JVM generator cannot see. This key does the
 ;; opposite bookkeeping for a var the CLJS PROBE cannot see: a
 ;; `:classification` row on a probe-covered `.cljc` namespace whose var is
 ;; defined under `#?(:clj …)` with NO ClojureScript arm at all. The row is
 ;; real — the generator introspects the live JVM var and owns its tier and
 ;; kind — but the probe reconciles every `:classification` row of a covered
 ;; namespace against the live CLJS surface, and a var that will never appear
 ;; there would report permanent staleness for behaving exactly as designed.
 ;;
 ;; The hatch is ONE-DIRECTIONAL by construction, which is what keeps it from
 ;; becoming a hole. Only the probe's direction 1 (rowed-but-not-live) skips
 ;; the entry; direction 2 (live-but-not-rowed) still covers the namespace
 ;; fully, so a var listed here that ever DID acquire a ClojureScript arm
 ;; would appear as a live public with no row and turn the probe RED.
 ;;
 ;; Not every JVM-only var on a covered door needs an entry: a
 ;; `#?(:clj (defmacro …))` reads as JVM-only in the source but, when it is
 ;; `:require-macros`-referred, the CLJS analyzer does carry it and it
 ;; reconciles normally. An entry is needed only where the CLJS host has
 ;; NOTHING.
 ;;
 ;; Each entry states its justification. Keep it short.
 :jvm-only-classification
 #{}

